mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 4 (Safe recreation and destruction)
Why: nvkmd lets a caller free a memory object while a VA still binds it.
 That is ordinary Vulkan — vkFreeMemory does not require every image
 bound to the memory to have been destroyed first — and Mesa's own WSI
 does exactly it: wsi_destroy_image calls FreeMemory before DestroyImage
 (wsi_common.c). horizon_gpu's contract is stricter and deliberately so:
 horizon_gpu_mem_destroy refuses with ERR_BUSY while any mapping of the
 object is alive (memory-model § 7).
 With no reference from the binding the two rules collide and the memory
 leaks. The free is refused, nothing retries it — nvkmd_mem_unref
 returns void and there is nowhere to report to — and the object keeps
 its storage and its NvMap for the rest of the process.
 With the reference, the free drops the caller's and returns, the unbind
 drops this one, and whichever comes last destroys the object. Order
 stops mattering, which is what the caller was entitled to assume.
 The one exception is the memory's own VA: every nvkmd_mem here carries
 a VA of its own, allocated into nvkmd_mem::va and bound at offset 0 the
 moment it is created, and referencing the memory from that binding
 makes the two objects hold each other — the refcount never reaches
 zero, the free never runs, the VA is never released, the binding never
 lets go. The exception is exact rather than a guess about ordering:
 alloc_mem assigns mem->va and then binds, so `_va == _mem->va`
 identifies the self-binding at the moment bind_mem runs. Every other
 VA belongs to somebody else — an image plane's, a sparse bind's — and
 those are the ones that outlive a vkFreeMemory and need to keep the
 memory alive.
Evidence: HARDWARE. t_vk_swapchain on the owner's console, 2026-08-05:
 without the reference, every swapchain image leaked — five swapchains,
 fourteen images — each one "destroy refused, 1 live mapping(s)", and
 vkDestroyDevice then refused outright with "device destroy refused:
 live mem=14 va_ranges=0 mappings=0 channels=0"
 (docs/hw-logs/t_vk_swapchain-run1-*.log). With the reference but
 without the self-binding exception (the intermediate state, patch 0058
 of the pre-compaction series): "device destroy refused: live mem=33
 va_ranges=33 mappings=33 channels=0" — the VAs leaking too is the
 signature of the cycle
 (docs/hw-logs/t_vk_swapchain-run2-refcycle-FAIL.log). The cycle fix
 was patch 0060; run 4 shows "device destroy refused" absent from the
 whole log.
Upstream: no — it is this backend's ownership rules meeting nvkmd's.

Absorbs (2026-08-23 series compaction): 0058 "a VA binding holds a
reference to its memory" and 0060 "do not reference a memory object
from its own VA".
---
 .../vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_va.c   | 60 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 60 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_va.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_va.c
index c543c25..9ffe7c4 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_va.c
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_va.c
@@ -30,6 +30,36 @@ struct nvkmd_horizon_binding {
    uint64_t va_offset_B;
    uint64_t range_B;
    horizon_gpu_mapping *mapping;
+
+   /* A REFERENCE, and it is what makes the two layers' ownership rules
+    * agree.
+    *
+    * nvkmd lets a caller free a memory object while a VA still binds
+    * it — that is ordinary Vulkan, since vkFreeMemory does not require
+    * every image bound to the memory to have been destroyed first, and
+    * Mesa's own WSI does exactly that (wsi_destroy_image calls
+    * FreeMemory before DestroyImage). horizon_gpu's contract is
+    * stricter and deliberately so: horizon_gpu_mem_destroy refuses with
+    * ERR_BUSY while any mapping of the object is alive
+    * (memory-model § 7).
+    *
+    * NULL for a memory object's own VA: see bind_mem for why that
+    * exception is not optional.
+    *
+    * Without a reference here the two rules collide and the memory
+    * leaks: the free is refused, nothing retries it, and the object
+    * stays alive with its storage and its NvMap for the rest of the
+    * process. Measured on hardware — fourteen swapchain images leaked
+    * in one run of t_vk_swapchain, and vkDestroyDevice then refused to
+    * tear the device down at all ("device destroy refused: live
+    * mem=14").
+    *
+    * With it, the free drops the caller's reference and returns, the
+    * unbind drops this one, and whichever comes last destroys the
+    * object. Order stops mattering, which is what the caller was
+    * entitled to assume.
+    */
+   struct nvkmd_mem *mem;
 };
 
 void
@@ -152,6 +182,12 @@ nvkmd_horizon_va_free(struct nvkmd_va *_va)
                             &va->bindings, link) {
       horizon_gpu_vm_unmap(b->mapping);
       list_del(&b->link);
+      /* Last, and after the unmap: this may be the reference that
+       * destroys the object, and horizon_gpu_mem_destroy refuses while
+       * the mapping is still alive. NULL for a memory object's own VA,
+       * which holds no reference — see bind_mem. */
+      if (b->mem != NULL)
+         nvkmd_mem_unref(b->mem);
       FREE(b);
     }
 
@@ -214,6 +250,28 @@ nvkmd_horizon_va_bind_mem(struct nvkmd_va *_va,
    b->va_offset_B = va_offset_B;
    b->range_B = range_B;
 
+   /* NOT FOR THE MEMORY'S OWN VA, and that exception is the whole
+    * difference between a reference and a cycle.
+    *
+    * Every nvkmd_mem here carries a VA of its own, allocated into
+    * nvkmd_mem::va and bound at offset 0 the moment it is created
+    * (nvkmd_horizon_mem.c). Referencing the memory from that binding
+    * makes the two objects hold each other: the mem's refcount never
+    * reaches zero, so its free never runs, so the VA is never released,
+    * so the binding never lets go. Measured on hardware — the first
+    * version of this reference leaked 33 memory objects, 33 VAs and 33
+    * mappings where the bug it was fixing had leaked 14 objects and no
+    * VAs at all. A fix that makes the number worse is worth saying out
+    * loud.
+    *
+    * mem->va is already assigned when this runs — alloc_mem sets it and
+    * then binds — so the comparison is exact rather than a guess about
+    * ordering. Every other VA is somebody else's: an image plane's, a
+    * sparse bind's. Those are the ones that outlive a vkFreeMemory and
+    * the ones that need to keep the memory alive.
+    */
+   b->mem = (_va == _mem->va) ? NULL : nvkmd_mem_ref(_mem);
+
    simple_mtx_lock(&va->mutex);
    list_addtail(&b->link, &va->bindings);
    simple_mtx_unlock(&va->mutex);
@@ -276,6 +334,8 @@ nvkmd_horizon_va_unbind(struct nvkmd_va *_va,
                        va_offset_B, (int)res.status, res.nv);
    }
 
+   if (found->mem != NULL)
+      nvkmd_mem_unref(found->mem);
    FREE(found);
    return VK_SUCCESS;
 }
